ntation></col><col><mnemonic>stack</mnemonic><name>Backtrace</name><engineering-type>tagged-backtrace</engineering-type><documentation>The backtrace is extracted from the state of the thread's stack, at the time the sample is taken. By walking backwards from the current call frame to the previous call frame, and so on, Instruments builds a path from the threads current location back to the main function for that thread. Backtraces are useful for exploring the flow control of your application. They describe how the processor eventually arrived at the instruction currently running at the time the sample was taken (i.e. the value of the PC, or program counter, register). The PC value is always added to the top, furthest from the main function, of the backtrace in a time profile. The Profile detail view shows these backtraces combined together into a call tree, so you can see the areas of your code that are most often on the CPU, during the selected time interval, at least statically. That backtraces in the list view are the instantaneous snapshots at the time of the sample.</documentation></col></schema><row><sample-time id="1" fmt="00:00.477.450">477450916</sample-time><thread id="2" fmt="CpuProbeWorker (0x623e520) (CpuProbe, pid: 92556)"><tid id="3" fmt="0x623e520">103015712</tid><process id="4" fmt="CpuProbe (92556)"><pid id="5" fmt="92556">92556</pid><device-session id="6" fmt="TODO">TODO</device-session></process></thread><process ref="4"/><core id="7" fmt="CPU 7 (P Core)">7</core><thread-state id="8" fmt="Running">Running</thread-state><weight id="9" fmt="1.00 ms">1000000</weight><tagged-backtrace id="10"><frame id="11" name="_platform_memmove" addr="0x1045a3d75"><binary id="12" name="libsystem_platform.dylib" UUID="284A4CD2-A934-3BA7-9277-47F5EF53D245" arch="arm64" load-addr="0x1045a0000" path="/usr/lib/system/libsystem_platform.dylib"/></frame><frame id="13" name="protocol witness for Collection.formIndex(after:) in conformance &lt;&gt; Range&lt;A&gt;" addr="0x